          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "I've explored enough to answer your first question directly. Here's what I found.\n\n## Does the app try to handle language in the Google login?\n\n**Yes — partially.** The Kompakt Google login runs in an embedded WebView (`KompaktOAuthWebViewActivity`), because the e-ink device has no Chrome/Custom Tabs. The auth request is built in `KompaktOAuthGoogle.signIn()`, and it does set the language:\n\n```kotlin\n// KompaktGoogleLoginViewModel.signIn()\nlocale = Locale.getDefault().toLanguageTag()   // e.g. \"pl-PL\"\n// → KompaktOAuthGoogle.signIn(): builder.setUiLocales(locale)\n```\n\n`setUiLocales(...)` puts a `ui_locales=pl-PL` parameter on the Google authorization URL — the standard OpenID Connect way to ask Google to render its login/consent pages in that language. So the intent is there.\n\n**What's *not* handled (the likely gaps):**\n\n1. **The WebView itself sets no `Accept-Language`.** `KompaktOAuthWebViewActivity.createWebView()` configures only the User-Agent, JavaScript, and cookies. Google's account pages lean heavily on the `Accept-Language` HTTP header in addition to `ui_locales`; the WebView falls back to whatever the device WebView build defaults to.\n2. **There's an `Accept-Language` interceptor in the codebase (`UserAgentInterceptor`), but it only applies to the app's own DAV/OkHttp traffic — not the WebView.** So it doesn't help here.\n3. **`ui_locales` alone is a hint Google may not honor across every page** of the multi-step sign-in; its own `hl` query param is the more direct lever.\n\nSo the picture is: the app asks once (`ui_locales`), but doesn't reinforce it where the WebView/Google actually decide rendering language.\n\nBefore I propose fixes, I need to pin down the root cause — and one observation splits the diagnosis cleanly in two:",
