          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "That confirms the diagnosis cleanly: **`Locale.getDefault()` is correct** (your native screens follow the phone language), so the problem is entirely on the **WebView ↔ Google side**. The app sets `ui_locales=<lang>`, but Google's login pages still render English on this device.\n\nThe reason this happens: Google's account pages decide their language from a mix of the `ui_locales` hint, the **`Accept-Language` HTTP header**, and Google's own `hl` URL parameter. `ui_locales` is a one-shot hint Google often drops after the first page; the embedded WebView on a de-googled build advertises an English-ish `Accept-Language`; and nothing pins `hl`. So English wins.\n\nHere are the approaches I see:\n\n**Approach 1 — Add Google's `hl` parameter to the auth URL (my recommendation).**\n`hl` is Google's *own* UI-language switch. Every `accounts.google.com` page reads it, and Google carries it through its internal redirects, so the **whole** sign-in flow stays in the chosen language — unlike `ui_locales`. One-line change in `KompaktOAuthGoogle.signIn()` via AppAuth's `setAdditionalParameters(mapOf(\"hl\" to lang))`. Device-independent (it's in the URL, not dependent on the WebView build). Keeps `ui_locales` as well.\n\n**Approach 2 — Set the WebView's `Accept-Language` header.**\nMake the WebView advertise the phone language. Problem: Android WebView has no public Accept-Language setter; the only hook is `loadUrl(url, headers)`, which applies *only* to the first main-frame request, not to the redirects/navigations in Google's multi-page login. Fragile for this flow, more code. Not a good primary fix.\n\n**Approach 3 — Both (`hl` + Accept-Language on initial load).**\nBelt-and-suspenders; most robust against an uncooperative WebView, but more moving parts — arguably premature until we confirm Approach 1 isn't enough.\n\nMy recommendation is **Approach 1**: smallest change, targets Google's most reliable lever, and it's testable on your device in minutes. If Google *still* overrides it there, we escalate to adding the `Accept-Language` header (Approach 3).",
