            "content": "# Localize the Google OAuth login WebView (SHP-570)\n\n**Date:** 2026-06-29\n**Branch:** `task/SHP-570` (from `kompakt`)\n**Status:** Design — approved for spec write\n\n## Problem\n\nOn the Mudita Kompakt, linking or re-authorizing a Google account opens an embedded\nWebView (`KompaktOAuthWebViewActivity`) hosting Google's sign-in/consent pages. Those\npages render in **English** even when the phone language is set to something else.\n\nThe rest of DAVx⁵'s own UI is correctly localized to the phone language — confirmed on\ndevice — so `Locale.getDefault()` is accurate. The problem is isolated to the\nWebView ↔ Google interaction.\n\n## Does the app already try to handle this?\n\nYes, partially. `KompaktGoogleLoginViewModel.signIn()` passes\n`locale = Locale.getDefault().toLanguageTag()` into `KompaktOAuthGoogle.signIn()`, which\ncalls AppAuth's `.setUiLocales(locale)`. This adds the OpenID Connect `ui_locales`\nparameter to the authorization URL.\n\n`ui_locales` is only a *hint*: Google applies it to the first page and frequently drops it\nacross the remaining steps of the multi-page sign-in. The embedded WebView (a stripped\nde-googled build with no Chrome/Custom Tabs) advertises an English-leaning\n`Accept-Language`, and nothing pins Google's own `hl` language switch. Net result: English\nwins after the first page.\n\n## Approach (chosen)\n\n**Add Google's `hl` URL parameter to the authorization request, alongside the existing\n`ui_locales`.**\n\n`hl` is Google's own UI-language switch. Every `accounts.google.com` page reads it, and\nGoogle carries it through its internal redirects, so the whole flow stays in the chosen\nlanguage. It lives in the URL, so it does not depend on the WebView build's\n`Accept-Language` behavior.\n\nAlternatives considered and rejected for now:\n\n- **Set the WebView's `Accept-Language` header.** Android WebView has no public\n  `Accept-Language` setter; the only hook (`loadUrl(url, headers)`) applies the header to\n  the initial main-frame request only, not to the navigations/redirects in Google's\n  multi-page login. Fragile, more code. Kept as a documented fallback (see below).\n- **Both `hl` + `Accept-Language`.** Belt-and-suspenders, but premature until on-device\n  testing shows `hl` alone is insufficient (YAGNI).\n\n## The change\n\n**Single file:** `core/src/main/kotlin/at/bitfire/davdroid/network/KompaktOAuthGoogle.kt`,\nin `signIn()`.\n\nAttach `hl` as an additional parameter when a locale is present:\n\n```kotlin\nval builder = AuthorizationRequest.Builder(\n    serviceConfig,\n    customClientId ?: CLIENT_ID,\n    ResponseTypeValues.CODE,\n    oAuthIntegration.redirectUri\n)\n    .setScopes(*SCOPES)\n    .setLoginHint(email)\n    .setUiLocales(locale)\nif (locale != null)\n    builder.setAdditionalParameters(mapOf(\"hl\" to locale))   // Google's own UI-language switch\nreturn builder.build()\n```\n\nNotes:\n\n- `hl` is a **custom** (non-OIDC) parameter, so AppAuth's `setAdditionalParameters`\n  accepts it. AppAuth only rejects reserved/built-in params (`ui_locales`, `scope`,\n  `client_id`, …); `hl` is not among them.\n- **`hl` value:** reuse the same `locale` string already passed in — the full BCP-47 tag\n  from `Locale.getDefault().toLanguageTag()` (e.g. `pl-PL`, `pt-BR`). The full tag\n  preserves regional variants the app ships (`pt` vs `pt-rBR` → `pt-BR` vs `pt-PT`);\n  Google tolerates the region suffix and maps unknown regions down to the base language.\n- `ui_locales` is **kept** — no behavior is removed.\n\n## Data flow (otherwise unchanged)\n\n```\nKompaktGoogleLoginViewModel.signIn()\n  locale = Locale.getDefault().toLanguageTag()\n    → KompaktOAuthGoogle.signIn()           // builds request with ui_locales + hl\n      → KompaktOAuthWebViewActivity          // loads request.toUri() (now carries hl)\n```\n\nBoth the **initial link** flow (`KompaktGoogleLogin.LoginScreen`) and the **re-auth** flow\n(`KompaktReauthScreen` → `KompaktGoogleLogin.LoginScreen`) call this same `signIn()`, so\none change covers both.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Upstream `OAuthGoogle.kt` / `OAuthFastmail.kt`** — these drive the Custom Tabs path,\n  which is not used on the device. Left untouched per the Kompakt\n  no-upstream-edits convention.\n- **`KompaktOAuthWebViewActivity`** — no WebView or `Accept-Language` changes.\n\n## Fallback (not implemented now)\n\nIf on-device testing shows Google *still* forces English despite `hl`, escalate to also\nsetting the WebView's `Accept-Language` on the initial load in\n`KompaktOAuthWebViewActivity` (the original \"Approach 3\"). Document and add it only then.\n\n## Success criteria\n\n1. `./gradlew :core:compileDebugKotlin` passes.\n2. On a device with phone language set to a shipped, Google-supported language (e.g.\n   Polish): *Link account* → the Google login WebView renders in that language. Same when\n   re-authorizing an existing account.\n3. The built authorization URL contains `hl=<tag>` (verifiable via a one-off log of\n   `request.toUri()` if needed).\n",
